1. What is Auditing?
Imagine your teacher keeps a notebook for homework:
Homework: Mathematics
Created on: 07-Aug-2026
Created by: Teacher
Last changed: 08-Aug-2026
Changed by: Principal
The actual homework is one thing.
But the notebook also remembers:
When was it created? When was it last changed? Who created it? Who changed it?
That's Auditing.
In a database, suppose we have:
Employee
-------------------------
id
name
email
salary

createdAt
updatedAt
createdBy
updatedBy

Spring Data JPA Auditing is a feature used to automatically track metadata about an entity,
such as when it was created, when it was last modified, who created it, and who last modified it.
Remember four things:
AUDITING
   ↓
WHEN created?
WHEN modified?
WHO created?
WHO modified?


Why Do We Need Auditing?
We need auditing to automatically track important information about changes made to database records.
For example, if an employee's salary changes from ₹50,000 to ₹70,000, later we may want to know:
When was the employee record created?
When was it last updated?
Who created it?
Who last modified it?
Without auditing, we would have to manage this information manually.

This is useful in:
Banking applications
E-commerce
HR systems
Admin dashboards
Order systems
Enterprise applications

4. The Four Main Annotations
Spring Data gives us:
@CreatedDate
@LastModifiedDate
@CreatedBy
@LastModifiedBy

Lifetime trick
TIME
 ├── @CreatedDate
 └── @LastModifiedDate

USER
 ├── @CreatedBy
 └── @LastModifiedBy

Example:
@Entity
@EntityListeners(AuditingEntityListener.class)
public class Employee {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    private String name;

    private Double salary;

    @CreatedDate
    private LocalDateTime createdAt;

    @LastModifiedDate
    private LocalDateTime updatedAt;
}

What is @EntityListeners?
@EntityListeners(AuditingEntityListener.class)
Think of the listener as someone watching the entity:
Listener = Watch entity events.
@EntityListeners is a JPA annotation used to tell JPA:
“Whenever certain lifecycle events happen to this entity, call this listener class.

9. We Must Enable Auditing?
In a Spring configuration class, auditing must be enabled:
@Configuration
@EnableJpaAuditing
